            <ArticleTitle>Diclofenac intramuscular single dose to decrease pain in post operative Caesarean section: a double blind randomized controlled trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>15-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the use of diclofenac intramuscular single dose to decrease pain in post operative Caesarean section with none used.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A double blind randomized controlled trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Ranong Hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixty-four patients who underwent post operative Caesarean section in Ranong Hospital from October 2003 to March 2004.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">The subjects were randomized by allocation to receive diclofenac intramuscular or placebo, both groups received morphine by Patient Controlled Analgesia (PCA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Amount of morphine sulfate used, in both groups by Patient Controlled Analgesia (PCA) and level of pain using Visual Analog Score (VAscore).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Morphine was used significantly less in the group of patients who had diclofenac intramuscular single dose in post operative Caesarean section but the level of pain was not significantly different.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A single dose of diclofenac intramuscularly decreases the use of morphine during the in post operative period of Caesarean section.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
